bhyve: nvme: the pin-based interrupt, and INTMS/INTMC

The emulated NVMe controller offered only MSI-X: it requested no legacy
interrupt and ignored writes to INTMS and INTMC.  A guest that cannot use
MSI-X could not attach the disk at all.  FreeBSD/arm64 booted with ACPI on
a GICv3 without an ITS is one: nvme(4) reports "unable to allocate MSI-X",
then "unable to allocate shared interrupt", and the attach fails.

Request a legacy interrupt, and while MSI-X is disabled drive it as the
specification describes for pin-based interrupts: asserted while a
completion queue with interrupts enabled holds an entry the guest has not
consumed and INTMS does not mask it; re-evaluated when a completion is
posted, when the guest moves a completion queue head doorbell, and when it
writes INTMS or INTMC.  With MSI-X enabled nothing changes.

